ution scores across many examples can help get a more global understanding of the model's behaviour, and has been used in previous work [49], this method is still limited. We therefore take inspiration from the \"Do Language Models Know the Way to Rome?\" [31] paper's analysis to get a more global understanding of Ember-V1 and its knowledge of marker genes. For each dataset, we compute the average cosine similarity between Ember-V1 embeddings of the top marker gene name for each cell type from PanglaoDB [20] and other marker genes from the same cell type, and compare this to the average cosine similarity between embeddings of these top markers and marker genes of different cell types. We then average results over all cell types. [truncated]","original_chars":17030},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Data collection and transformation","heading_level":3,"line_start":59,"line_end":62,"text":"We focus our experiments on the datasets that were used to evaluate the cell type classification and clustering performance of GenePT [8], as well as the subsample of the Tabula Sapiens dataset [14] used to evaluate CELLama [10]. For each dataset, we use the same train/test split as each of these respective works to evaluate performance on downstream tasks. Cells were represented using cell sentences following the same approach as GenePT-s [8], where gene names are listed in descending order of expression level, omitting genes with zero counts. These cell sentences are then passed to text encoders to generate cell embeddings, or to generative LLMs for cell type classification through autoregressive generation.","original_chars":719},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"scMPT vs scGPT F1 Score on Different Datasets","heading_level":3,"line_start":414,"line_end":429,"text":"Figure 3: Comparison between cell type classification F1 score of scMPT and scGPT on all datasets studied. scMPT outperforms scGPT on most datasets tested, with particularly significant improvements on the Pancreas and Bones datasets.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nWe also test the performance of scMPT on disease (specifically, aneurysm) phenotype prediction using the Aorta dataset. We use the same train and test split as the one used to evaluate cell type classification.\n\nTable 20: scMPT disease phenotype prediction performance vs unimodal models - Aorta dataset.\n\n| Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1 |\n|----------------------------------|-----------------|-----------------|-----------------|-----------------------|\n| scMPT Ember-V1 + MLP scGPT + MLP | 0.894 (0.00197) | 0.840 (0.00434) | 0.822 (0.00138) | 0.831 (0.00216) 0.799 |\n| | 0.863 | 0.809 | 0.790 | |\n| | 0.862 | 0.810 | 0.786 | 0.797 |","original_chars":900},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":2,"line_start":57,"line_end":84,"text":"### 3.1 Data collection and transformation\n\nWe focus our experiments on the datasets that were used to evaluate the cell type classification and clustering performance of GenePT [8], as well as the subsample of the Tabula Sapiens dataset [14] used to evaluate CELLama [10]. For each dataset, we use the same train/test split as each of these respective works to evaluate performance on downstream tasks. Cells were represented using cell sentences following the same approach as GenePT-s [8], where gene names are listed in descending order of expression level, omitting genes with zero counts. These cell sentences are then passed to text encoders to generate cell embeddings, or to generative LLMs for cell type classification through autoregressive generation.\n\n### 3.2 Cell embedding approaches\n\nIn general, text encoders can vary greatly in performance on tasks such as classification and clustering. To determine what LLM of this type to use for our experiments, we therefore test a variety of pre-trained models, selected based on Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB) performance [37]. We use the same experimental design and metrics as the GenePT paper to evaluate classification and clustering performance [8]. For example, using a 10-nearest neighbours method for zero-shot cell type classification. Details can be found in Appendix A.1. Overall, we find that Ember-V1 outperforms text embedding models used in previous work for generating cell embeddings from cell sentences, motivating us to select this encoder for our experiments. [truncated]","original_chars":7557}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001161.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001161","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a new model paper about scMPT for single-cell analysis, not a review or benchmark. The provided sections only show gene-name “cell sentences” encoded by text models, which are biological tokens rather than a substantive natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“Cells were represented using cell sentences ... where gene names are listed in descending order of expression level” / “passed to text encoders ... or to generative LLMs for cell type classification”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001161","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The paper introduces scMPT as a new fusion model, and the methods explicitly use generative LLMs with autoregressive generation. It does not clearly state that scMPT itself is broadly pretrained or a foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“cells are then passed to text encoders to generate cell embeddings, or to generative LLMs for cell type classification through autor